WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.520
- Schools reopening should
not be a politicized topic.

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.360
We should let science guide us to make

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:07.110
the best possible decision.

00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:08.810
It's not gonna be a perfect one,

00:00:08.810 --> 00:00:10.560
but it's gonna be the best one we can do

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:13.960
in our given situation and adapt as we go.

00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:16.070
Here I sit down with Dr. David Epstein

00:00:16.070 --> 00:00:17.820
to talk about schools' reopening,

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.010
who is a pediatric ICU physician,

00:00:20.010 --> 00:00:22.420
who also works in a pediatric urgent care

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:25.570
right on the front lines
during COVID-19, enjoy.

00:00:25.570 --> 00:00:27.640
Dr. Epstein, I wanna
welcome you to the channel.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:29.000
Tell me your background,

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.900
tell me why you would be
one of the best people,

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.680
if not the best person to
talk about this subject.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.120
- Well, thank you, Dr. Varshavski,
I really appreciate you

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.720
inviting me to the conversation.

00:00:39.640 --> 00:00:43.160
Well, I'm a boarded in pediatrics

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:45.460
and I did subspecialty training

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:47.443
in pediatric critical care medicine.

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:52.823
I worked a few years at UCLA
and then worked about 10,

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.300
14 years at Children's
Hospital, Los Angeles,

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.770
in the cardiothoracic ICU there.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:03.210
And over the last five years,
I opened up a pediatric

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:06.110
Urgent Care in which we've
been caring for children

00:01:06.110 --> 00:01:09.060
on the front lines with urgent care needs.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:11.810
So being exposed to a
lot of variety of illness

00:01:11.810 --> 00:01:14.910
in pediatrics, from those
that are minor to those that

00:01:14.910 --> 00:01:16.830
are quite life threatening.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.890
- Since we're gonna be
talking about the subject

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:21.170
of schools' reopening, I
guess the first thought

00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:24.210
that everyone has is, are
children gonna be safe

00:01:24.210 --> 00:01:25.910
because they're going to be
exposed to other children

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:27.650
who potentially could
be spreading this virus?

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:31.850
- California in general,
maybe about nine, eight or 9%

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:36.830
of the total positive
population has been children.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:39.010
- I wanna make a statement
and you correct me

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:42.760
if you think it's wrong, or
perhaps you'll agree with me.

00:01:42.760 --> 00:01:46.900
If I had a five year old
child and I was faced with

00:01:46.900 --> 00:01:49.410
this very weird hypothetical
situation where I had to choose

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.580
between them getting the
influenza virus or COVID-19,

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:55.160
I think I would want my
child to get COVID-19

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:57.740
'cause statistically, at least right now,

00:01:57.740 --> 00:01:59.730
it looks like they fare
better with the virus,

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:00.563
would you agree?

00:02:00.563 --> 00:02:03.240
- Of the kids less than 17
years of age, California

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.223
hasn't documented any deaths
less than 17 years of age.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.410
You know, most of the deaths have been

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.690
in the population greater
than 65 years of age,

00:02:12.690 --> 00:02:15.970
so I have to think of
it in terms of morbidity

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.400
and mortality, things like that,

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.840
and so from a mortality
standpoint, it seems like,

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:22.940
you know, kids, the population it's an,

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:25.410
it seems to be almost
negligible as far as mortality.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.580
When you talk about kids
who die from influenza,

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:31.700
that seems to be a very
rare event as well,

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.020
I mean, I think annually,
there's probably about 180 kids

00:02:35.020 --> 00:02:38.490
that die from influenza per year.

00:02:38.490 --> 00:02:42.050
And I'm sure the national
numbers for kids dying from COVID

00:02:42.050 --> 00:02:44.010
is not negligible, I'm
sure that, you know,

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:45.830
there are a number of cases.

00:02:45.830 --> 00:02:49.900
So you're trying to balance that,

00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.680
the concern is, and the anxiety is,

00:02:51.680 --> 00:02:54.640
is that you have a vaccine for influenza,

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:57.460
you have treatment for one, for influenza,

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:58.950
and you're still only having, you know,

00:02:58.950 --> 00:03:01.140
you're still having a
mortality of 186 children,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.440
which you feel is kind
of more preventable.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:05.580
When you're talking about COVID
you don't have any of the,

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.380
that safety net as far
as treatment or vaccine.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:12.180
I think the anxiety of not
knowing what could happen

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:15.390
is an issue because we're
worried about, you know,

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:19.440
kids who have, may have
underlying illnesses

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.500
like a juvenile idiopathic arthritis,

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.100
or they may have some
other immunodeficiency

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:28.040
or they may be immunocompromised
and they may not show up

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.300
on the mortality board,

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.880
but they may show up on the
morbidity board of getting

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.360
more severe disease and having
issues with their medications

00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:40.540
and having them step back while
the numbers don't look bad,

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:44.050
as far as mortality and
contagion and having kids passing

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:46.220
along to adults, I think some,

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:50.270
some anecdotal reports have
said that maybe the index case

00:03:50.270 --> 00:03:52.860
of children passing to
adults is probably 9%

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.810
or something like that
and so they don't seem

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:57.410
to be passing along for, you
know, the young, young kids.

00:03:57.410 --> 00:03:59.360
- I think your answer is
very fair that we don't know

00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:00.340
what that answer is.

00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:02.540
We can lean one way or
another in the moment,

00:04:02.540 --> 00:04:05.030
looking at the current
evidence that may shift

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:07.680
as more evidence comes
out because for example,

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:10.420
we see this low index
number that you mentioned,

00:04:10.420 --> 00:04:13.120
which is this how easily
children spread this virus

00:04:13.120 --> 00:04:15.490
and we think, okay, maybe
they're not spreading it as much.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:19.500
We see the lower number of
that multiinflammatory syndrome

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:22.150
being not so high and we
think, okay, not so bad,

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:25.310
but did those radically change
when we go back to school?

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:27.600
Very possible, right.
- Yeah, yeah

00:04:27.600 --> 00:04:29.130
we, and we don't know that yet

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:31.480
and I think, you know, a lot
of countries around the world

00:04:31.480 --> 00:04:34.900
are going back to school in
various forms or fashions

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.510
but we have to think
that, you know, it's only,

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.790
we're looking at something
that's only gone on for months,

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.570
you know, and the information
we have on influenza

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:45.610
is years, in the ICU, we like to know

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:47.620
what we're dealing with,
we don't like to know

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:50.014
about the unknown and so,
- Of course.

00:04:50.014 --> 00:04:51.510
- having that, you know,
things be a little bit

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.410
more concrete and no one is a little bit

00:04:53.410 --> 00:04:54.780
more comforting to us, control

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:55.640
we're controlled first.
- Obvious.

00:04:55.640 --> 00:04:57.280
Yeah, as physicians we oftentimes

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.470
have to make recommendations
with limited evidence,

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:01.240
that happens quite often, you know,

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:04.109
a B or C level recommendation

00:05:04.109 --> 00:05:04.970
and we have to give that to our patients

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.540
because that's what we
know in a given moment.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.370
What I think is gonna
happen is in some way,

00:05:09.370 --> 00:05:11.950
we're gonna return back to
school or return back to normal.

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:15.250
And we have to be flexible
enough and adaptable enough

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:17.320
to pivot at any given moment.

00:05:17.320 --> 00:05:21.210
That's why in my stance,
it makes sense to either,

00:05:21.210 --> 00:05:24.470
A, delay school until
we have more information

00:05:24.470 --> 00:05:27.110
and not be hard set by
these deadlines where,

00:05:27.110 --> 00:05:29.660
you know, by August 7th,
we have to have a decision.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.970
Well, if we don't have the
good info to guide us then,

00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:34.730
maybe we can push that a few
weeks and see what happens,

00:05:34.730 --> 00:05:37.570
because ultimately we're
tryna play this game of risks

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:41.130
and balances without all
the cards on the table.

00:05:41.130 --> 00:05:44.120
And perhaps if we buy ourselves two weeks,

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:47.150
if we fit, allow our study to be finished,

00:05:47.150 --> 00:05:50.010
we'll be able to make a
better decision for schools,

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:52.520
for parents, for teachers,
all of the above.

00:05:52.520 --> 00:05:57.170
What are your thoughts on
teacher safety and all of the

00:05:57.170 --> 00:05:59.780
conversation that's
surrounding teachers being safe

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:01.750
around children in COVID-19 ara?

00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:04.540
- I think in general,
pediatricians are in favor

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.093
of going back to school
in some form or fashion.

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:11.090
While we take into account
the pediatric standpoint,

00:06:11.090 --> 00:06:13.820
oftentimes I think we
forget about the teachers

00:06:13.820 --> 00:06:15.130
who are the adults in the classroom

00:06:15.130 --> 00:06:17.240
and different countries around the world

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:19.390
have had some experience

00:06:19.390 --> 00:06:23.230
and I know we hear
about Israel and Germany

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:25.500
and, you know, in Israel,
there was a lot of contagion

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:28.200
and they had to shut back
down and there were teachers

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:30.750
who were affected and you know kids

00:06:32.090 --> 00:06:33.810
in Germany, I think there was less so,

00:06:33.810 --> 00:06:37.363
I think it was more a
spread by children and,

00:06:38.219 --> 00:06:40.653
or spread amongst kids
and not so much staff.

00:06:42.180 --> 00:06:43.820
Every country seems to be
a little bit different,

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.610
I think they we're still learning

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:48.850
and I do think that the teachers have

00:06:48.850 --> 00:06:51.070
a voice in all of this,
because it's their lives

00:06:51.070 --> 00:06:53.170
that are on line and some
of them may be elderly.

00:06:53.170 --> 00:06:56.100
- We have to be honest and
talk about the disparities

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:58.920
in socioeconomic factors that
some schools are dealing with

00:06:58.920 --> 00:07:01.010
a lot less resources than others.

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:03.730
I've been, my entire childhood, I've,

00:07:03.730 --> 00:07:06.930
went to inner city
schools and I've seen how

00:07:06.930 --> 00:07:08.950
they've had to manage with low resources.

00:07:08.950 --> 00:07:11.010
And I can't imagine some
of these things that are

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:13.460
on the CDC checklist being accomplished.

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:15.440
While I think it's great
and the CDC put out

00:07:15.440 --> 00:07:17.600
the correct recommendations,

00:07:17.600 --> 00:07:20.190
just from a realistic standpoint,
where are you gonna get

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:22.800
these employees that are gonna
be doing contact tracing?

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:25.960
Where are you going to be
getting substitute teachers from

00:07:25.960 --> 00:07:28.730
when teachers who are
elderly can't come in

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:31.680
or when someone has an
immunocompromised state

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.500
or checking on students who didn't come in

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:35.020
because their parents are sick?

00:07:35.020 --> 00:07:38.130
It just, there's so many
variables that it's impossible

00:07:38.130 --> 00:07:39.700
to get a perfect scenario.

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:42.430
So we have to lose that idea
of perfection right away,

00:07:42.430 --> 00:07:46.270
and be open to this nuance
conversation where perhaps

00:07:46.270 --> 00:07:48.500
there's not a one size fits all solution,

00:07:48.500 --> 00:07:50.170
especially on a geographic level,

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:52.990
like what works in an area
where there's low infectivity

00:07:52.990 --> 00:07:55.840
versus where it's peaking has
to be completely different.

00:07:55.840 --> 00:07:59.030
I wanna ask you a pointed,
very difficult question,

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:01.380
what is your stance on schools reopening?

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:04.070
- Yeah, no, I think that's
a very good question

00:08:04.070 --> 00:08:06.030
and I, it's something that
I've been thinking about a lot,

00:08:06.030 --> 00:08:08.320
because I have school aged
children and you know,

00:08:08.320 --> 00:08:11.130
they're going back, you know,
need to go back to school

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:16.130
and I vote for going back to school,

00:08:16.780 --> 00:08:17.970
bottom line.
- For what reasons,

00:08:17.970 --> 00:08:18.820
specifically?

00:08:18.820 --> 00:08:20.170
- Okay, well, this is good.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:24.440
So, I agree with everything that you said

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:28.440
in as much as every area of the country

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:29.273
is going to be different.

00:08:29.273 --> 00:08:32.710
What works in, you know,
the middle of Montana

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:36.204
is not gonna work in the
middle of Los Angeles, yeah or

00:08:36.204 --> 00:08:38.220
you know, or Los Angeles County, so it,

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:40.410
there are different geographic areas.

00:08:40.410 --> 00:08:43.475
There are different places
that have different incidents

00:08:43.475 --> 00:08:47.570
of disease and so the precautions
will likely be different

00:08:47.570 --> 00:08:50.660
and we have to shape those
accordingly and like you said,

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:53.140
there are disparities in
districts and it's gonna be hard

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.140
to accommodate all those recommendations

00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:57.160
which are the right thing.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:00.600
The reason why I believe
that kids need to be

00:09:00.600 --> 00:09:03.080
going back to school, the important things

00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:07.360
are, A, the education and I
know that in some districts,

00:09:07.360 --> 00:09:12.030
they may not have the ability
to do remote learning,

00:09:12.030 --> 00:09:14.810
you know, tele-education,
those kinds of things,

00:09:14.810 --> 00:09:16.473
as adequately as other areas.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:22.020
The other things are just the
socialization of children,

00:09:22.020 --> 00:09:27.020
children like respond
and map out their world,

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:30.410
depending on the contacts they make.

00:09:30.410 --> 00:09:33.530
Right now, we're creating like
a two dimensional environment

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:37.440
for our kids and for us
and really as human beings,

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:40.200
we develop in a three dimensional world,

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:41.720
with that contact, with other people,

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:43.890
those exchanges or those kinds of things.

00:09:43.890 --> 00:09:46.460
And it's very difficult for
kids because their learning

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:48.490
and their development depends on that.

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:51.460
And you look at, I mean, one
of the things that I've seen

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:56.070
in the clinic is seeing
people, adolescents,

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:59.060
and this was near the
end of the school year,

00:09:59.060 --> 00:10:03.430
having physical manifestations
of their anxiety,

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:08.430
their depression, their
isolation from their peers,

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:11.730
not being able to go to prom,

00:10:11.730 --> 00:10:14.330
not being able to
graduate with their peers,

00:10:14.330 --> 00:10:17.000
not being able to interact in
this most important milestone

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:20.390
of their lives and granted,
you know, at that time,

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:23.130
that's a very, very important
thing in their life.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:26.580
And so going back to school
means a lot of those things

00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:29.693
and not going back to
school and isolation,

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:32.940
kind of, it promotes almost, it's almost

00:10:32.940 --> 00:10:34.200
like a traumatic event.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.140
You know, the educational aspects,

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:39.040
the trauma that develops from isolation,

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:41.850
I think those are very,
very real concerns.

00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:44.560
Obviously we have to balance
this with the medical aspects

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:49.560
and as a physician, I'm
extremely torn because I know

00:10:50.130 --> 00:10:52.310
the potential and having worked in the ICU

00:10:52.310 --> 00:10:55.330
and being in the clinic and
hearing about what's going,

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:57.370
what, when, what New York went through,

00:10:57.370 --> 00:11:00.540
I have images in my mind and understand

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:05.300
how severe things can get
and so trying to balance

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:09.770
those issues with the issues
of children, not going back

00:11:09.770 --> 00:11:13.023
to school, it's a very, very real thing,

00:11:14.409 --> 00:11:15.760
but I do advocate for
going back to school,

00:11:15.760 --> 00:11:19.223
but you mentioned one
important word in your,

00:11:20.700 --> 00:11:23.410
in your explanation of
why you felt, you know,

00:11:23.410 --> 00:11:25.830
going back or going back
to school was important,

00:11:25.830 --> 00:11:30.090
or how to go back to school
and that's the word pivot.

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:35.090
And I really liked that term
because we have to be flexible

00:11:35.130 --> 00:11:37.900
and we have to reassess.

00:11:37.900 --> 00:11:42.900
It's like the plan, do,
study, act, cycle, you know,

00:11:43.080 --> 00:11:44.220
of quality improvement.

00:11:44.220 --> 00:11:48.220
I mean, we have to assess
what we've done and we have to

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:51.350
modify depending on what the outcome is

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:55.040
and so it shouldn't be
like you a very concrete,

00:11:55.040 --> 00:11:58.660
you know, yes at all
costs, no at all costs.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:00.900
It has to be somewhere in between.

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:02.530
- You know I completely
agree with you on the topic

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:03.650
of opening schools.

00:12:03.650 --> 00:12:07.890
At the same time, if I'm
a teacher and I'm elderly,

00:12:07.890 --> 00:12:09.850
or I'm not elderly, or I
have someone in my home

00:12:09.850 --> 00:12:13.670
that's elderly and I wanna
teach, it sounds like

00:12:13.670 --> 00:12:18.670
you're saying the children's
social educational development

00:12:18.950 --> 00:12:21.420
is more important than my safety.

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:22.460
How would you respond to that?

00:12:22.460 --> 00:12:24.030
I'm strictly playing
devil's advocate here.

00:12:24.030 --> 00:12:25.680
- That's a very hard thing,
I mean, I wouldn't wanna

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:28.103
put teachers' lives at risk by any means,

00:12:29.030 --> 00:12:32.330
you know, there's gonna
be more data as time goes.

00:12:32.330 --> 00:12:35.670
I would hope that schools
would find a way to be able

00:12:35.670 --> 00:12:38.530
to pivot or be able to do things in a way

00:12:38.530 --> 00:12:40.830
that can make it safe for teachers

00:12:40.830 --> 00:12:43.640
but I do think it's important
for kids to go back to school.

00:12:43.640 --> 00:12:47.890
And, you know, maybe if it
had, you know, maybe if it is,

00:12:47.890 --> 00:12:52.273
you know, some form of, you
know, mixed tele-education,

00:12:53.250 --> 00:12:57.150
you know, live education, I
know that a lot of countries

00:12:57.150 --> 00:12:59.090
and a lot of schools that are,

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:02.380
been planning, have been
doing planning pods, yeah,

00:13:02.380 --> 00:13:04.410
and shifts of when kids go back

00:13:04.410 --> 00:13:06.040
so you're creating smaller environments,

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:09.620
creating more distancing,
having kids wearing masks,

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:12.220
creating a, you know,
regular hand-washing,

00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:15.020
you know, screening kids
by, you know, temperature,

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:16.820
although that's not always accurate,

00:13:17.990 --> 00:13:20.820
having kids sit out who
are, who have been sick.

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:25.670
- Unfortunately we're forced
to be in a position of choosing

00:13:25.670 --> 00:13:29.410
the lesser of two evils,
almost like a rationing

00:13:29.410 --> 00:13:31.560
of care situation, which we obviously hate

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:32.840
in the healthcare space.

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:35.880
Basically rationing of care
is when you have two patients

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:37.000
and you only have one ventilator,

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:37.920
and you have to make the choice,

00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:40.070
who's more likely to benefit from this.

00:13:40.070 --> 00:13:42.630
That's almost what we're
doing when we're saying,

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:43.970
you know, should we open schools or not?

00:13:43.970 --> 00:13:46.030
'Cause we see the
detrimental effects to kids

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:49.710
on a mega scale and we see the
potential risk for teachers

00:13:49.710 --> 00:13:51.740
'cause at this moment
it's a potential risk,

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:54.300
we don't know it for certain
because we don't have the data

00:13:54.300 --> 00:13:55.510
to back that up.

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:57.570
So, we're sort of saying, okay, look,

00:13:57.570 --> 00:13:59.980
there is this potential danger,

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:01.920
but we know this other danger for children

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:04.610
most definitely exists, so let's start up

00:14:04.610 --> 00:14:06.370
in a gradual approach and build up

00:14:06.370 --> 00:14:09.070
and continually reassess our strategies.

00:14:09.070 --> 00:14:12.570
Will teachers have negative
outcomes from this?

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:15.330
It to some degree, yes, we
have to be honest about it.

00:14:15.330 --> 00:14:17.540
Will teachers catch
COVID-19 as schools reopen?

00:14:17.540 --> 00:14:19.360
Yes, we just have to say that.

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:21.030
Saying anything otherwise is a lie.

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:23.837
As our federal leaders go on
television, if they just say,

00:14:23.837 --> 00:14:25.410
"Oh, it's no big deal,
no one's gonna suffer,"

00:14:25.410 --> 00:14:26.970
that's a lie.

00:14:26.970 --> 00:14:29.190
The approach that I feel
has worked best for me

00:14:29.190 --> 00:14:32.280
in the healthcare space
has been the most upfront

00:14:32.280 --> 00:14:34.770
that you can be with what you're doing,

00:14:34.770 --> 00:14:37.980
why you're doing it and how you will pivot

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:39.510
if information changes.

00:14:39.510 --> 00:14:42.020
And once you can get the
people who you're treating

00:14:42.020 --> 00:14:44.870
in this situation,
teachers, on the same page

00:14:44.870 --> 00:14:47.000
as to why you're making these decisions,

00:14:47.000 --> 00:14:49.750
the more likely they are to
be, to accept the outcome.

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:51.790
And I feel that the federal government,

00:14:51.790 --> 00:14:54.480
the media has done a really
poor job arming teachers

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:57.160
with good information
about what risks they face,

00:14:57.160 --> 00:14:59.710
what mitigation strategies they can use,

00:14:59.710 --> 00:15:02.350
because some of the things
listed in the CDC manuals

00:15:02.350 --> 00:15:04.370
are just impossible for
many school districts,

00:15:04.370 --> 00:15:06.490
like the six foot rule is great.

00:15:06.490 --> 00:15:08.010
It's not even a perfect rule,

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:09.830
but it's not realistic for many schools.

00:15:09.830 --> 00:15:12.150
So perhaps we need to downgrade
to the three foot rule

00:15:12.150 --> 00:15:14.000
that the WHO set forth.

00:15:14.000 --> 00:15:16.270
Perhaps we could move
some classrooms outdoors,

00:15:16.270 --> 00:15:18.180
whether we see less transmission.

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.620
There's all these things
that are being pushed aside

00:15:20.620 --> 00:15:24.270
of the conversation in favor
of a political talking point

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:27.680
that really should not be
anywhere in this discussion.

00:15:27.680 --> 00:15:29.640
Politics should mean the least

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:31.560
when it comes to opening schools,

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:32.470
because at the end of the day,

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:35.260
whether you're the independent,
Republican, Democrat,

00:15:35.260 --> 00:15:38.510
your child is your child and
if you have a family member

00:15:38.510 --> 00:15:41.220
that has children, or has
a teacher in their family,

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:42.820
you should care equally for them.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:44.871
- Communication is really important

00:15:44.871 --> 00:15:46.610
and I think communication
with teachers is gonna be

00:15:46.610 --> 00:15:50.550
ultimately important and
trying to educate them

00:15:50.550 --> 00:15:55.200
with regards to what information
we have on COVID right now,

00:15:55.200 --> 00:15:58.040
which like you said, it's very imperfect

00:15:58.040 --> 00:16:00.920
and we're still learning
and we need to reassess

00:16:00.920 --> 00:16:03.450
but I think to have,

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:06.860
have two groups suffer,
needlessly, you know,

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:11.420
children and teachers is wrong.

00:16:11.420 --> 00:16:15.160
I think, and we have to
balance each of their,

00:16:15.160 --> 00:16:16.053
each of their,

00:16:17.210 --> 00:16:18.830
their needs

00:16:18.830 --> 00:16:21.790
and we have to reassess, I mean, it's a,

00:16:21.790 --> 00:16:23.800
it has to be a very balanced approach.

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:26.880
- Let's take a look sometime
and talk about the outliers

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:28.090
in this situation.

00:16:28.090 --> 00:16:30.360
You know, we've mentioned
social determinants of health,

00:16:30.360 --> 00:16:33.320
we mentioned lower
socioeconomic school districts.

00:16:33.320 --> 00:16:34.800
You know, there are schools out there

00:16:34.800 --> 00:16:36.980
that receive funding from property taxes

00:16:36.980 --> 00:16:39.560
in very wealthy neighborhoods
that can, you know,

00:16:39.560 --> 00:16:42.930
put acrylic across,
acrylic is that plastic

00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:45.560
see-through covering you
see in some of the stores

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:47.630
and medical offices, they
could put that across

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:49.785
the entire classroom.
- Okay.

00:16:49.785 --> 00:16:50.618
- And then there's some areas where

00:16:50.618 --> 00:16:51.520
that's gonna be completely impossible.

00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:54.440
There's some areas where
students rely on school

00:16:54.440 --> 00:16:56.030
for feeding their children.
- Yeah.

00:16:56.030 --> 00:16:58.240
- Some areas that require the schools

00:16:58.240 --> 00:16:59.940
so that they can get to work themselves

00:16:59.940 --> 00:17:01.580
because they don't have childcare options

00:17:01.580 --> 00:17:03.140
available to them.
- Yeah.

00:17:03.140 --> 00:17:07.300
- Unless we bring all of
these very important pieces

00:17:07.300 --> 00:17:09.150
of the conversation to the table,

00:17:09.150 --> 00:17:11.410
we're gonna be leaving
a significant population

00:17:11.410 --> 00:17:13.270
of the US behind.

00:17:13.270 --> 00:17:15.311
And that's not good either.

00:17:15.311 --> 00:17:18.308
I can easily see two
individuals on this subject,

00:17:18.308 --> 00:17:20.780
you know, a teacher on one
end who's immunocompromised

00:17:20.780 --> 00:17:22.470
and is worried about going back to school

00:17:22.470 --> 00:17:24.850
and at the same time, I can see a parent

00:17:24.850 --> 00:17:28.350
in a low socioeconomic neighborhood,

00:17:28.350 --> 00:17:30.427
the special needs child saying,

00:17:30.427 --> 00:17:32.728
"I need my child to go back to school,

00:17:32.728 --> 00:17:34.767
"I can't care for them without going broke

00:17:34.767 --> 00:17:36.997
"because I need to go to work"
or "I don't have the ability

00:17:36.997 --> 00:17:39.460
"to teach them because
I'm not trained in this."

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:43.350
And both sides are right
in what they're saying.

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:48.020
They both need to be heard,
but where do you go from there?

00:17:48.020 --> 00:17:51.074
That's where I struggle
myself even at times.

00:17:51.074 --> 00:17:51.907
- Well, I don't think you're alone,

00:17:51.907 --> 00:17:54.120
I mean, I think the whole
country is struggling with that.

00:17:54.120 --> 00:17:55.320
I think there are a lot of people,

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:58.620
I mean, if it was easy, you know,

00:17:58.620 --> 00:18:00.280
we wouldn't be having these discussions.

00:18:00.280 --> 00:18:02.740
I mean, really we wouldn't,
if everything was going

00:18:02.740 --> 00:18:05.700
the way it should be going,
but there are a lot of

00:18:05.700 --> 00:18:09.350
competing demands and trying
to prioritize one over another

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:13.910
sometimes is very difficult,
but we do have to invest

00:18:13.910 --> 00:18:17.960
in our children and you know,
the children are our future.

00:18:17.960 --> 00:18:20.100
- People watch this
conversation, you and I speak,

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:22.720
they understand where both
of you and I are coming from,

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:25.710
what is the one line you
wanna leave them with?

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:27.340
- Yeah, you know, I am,

00:18:28.340 --> 00:18:31.690
the things that we're doing
aren't hard to prevent this.

00:18:31.690 --> 00:18:35.373
And the one thing that
I, if we work together,

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:39.496
you know, we're gonna get
through this a lot easier

00:18:39.496 --> 00:18:42.310
and a lot quicker and so
if you're wearing a mask,

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:43.900
if you're doing simple things like trying

00:18:43.900 --> 00:18:46.530
to distance yourself, washing your hands,

00:18:46.530 --> 00:18:49.690
not touching your face, staying away

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:51.490
if you have a cold symptoms,

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:53.640
these are very, very simple things to do.

00:18:53.640 --> 00:18:56.670
There's a lot of controversy
surrounding these topics

00:18:56.670 --> 00:19:00.003
and a lot of pushback
on doing certain things,

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:03.523
because it's not what we're used to.

00:19:04.465 --> 00:19:06.309
It's not what we feel comfortable with.

00:19:06.309 --> 00:19:07.690
This is gonna be
something that's transient

00:19:07.690 --> 00:19:11.660
and the time that we invest
now on prevention and spread,

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:15.750
it's going to pay off in
dividends in the future

00:19:15.750 --> 00:19:19.084
because we'll be able to
go back to work the sooner,

00:19:19.084 --> 00:19:21.380
we'll be able to be comfortable
with having our kids

00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:24.740
in school sooner, without restrictions.

00:19:24.740 --> 00:19:25.700
You know, there's a lot of things,

00:19:25.700 --> 00:19:27.970
so the sooner that we can
implement these things

00:19:27.970 --> 00:19:31.653
and be more, work as a team,

00:19:32.950 --> 00:19:34.830
- Yeah.
- yeah, that, you know,

00:19:34.830 --> 00:19:36.410
we're gonna get through
this a lot quicker.

